E OutlinerIntent                       | Api/OutlinerSequence.h       | 34-45   | What the interface asked the outliner to do. Never applied where it arrives; applied at ① of a tick. `RevisionSequence` like any other edit. Drag-reordering that mutated the relation directly would bypass undo, and its absence from the sequence is discovered by the artist rather than by a test.
T DeclaredIntent                       | Api/OutlinerSequence.h       | 51-60   | One declared intent, complete enough to apply without consulting whoever declared it. ①. Every operand is named here so that what is applied is what was meant.
T RejectedIntent                       | Api/OutlinerSequence.h       | 71-75   | One rejected intent, with both operands named — what `86` presents. naming both occupants, and never applied. Both identities are held here rather than formatted into a message, so the reporting stays nonallocating and the presenter chooses the wording.
T OutlinerSequence                     | Api/OutlinerSequence.h       | 90-186  | Everything `12` owns, reconciled in the one order `12` §4 fixes. · ④ repair enclosure labels · ⑤ rebuild rows and adjust counts · ⑥ re-derive changed subsets · ⑦ re-derive search entries for renamed occupants. Every one of those orderings is load-bearing: ③ before ④ because transforms must be final before anything spatial is derived from them, and ④ before ⑤ because rows rebuilt against stale labels are briefly wrong and are displayed. completed tick left, and `Reconcile` is the only writer of it.
